1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:03,168
It is two in the morning, and Aziz's phone buzzes.

2
00:00:03,218 --> 00:00:05,040
A code from the bank.

3
00:00:05,090 --> 00:00:06,912
Then another one.

4
00:00:06,962 --> 00:00:08,688
He did not ask for any of them.

5
00:00:08,738 --> 00:00:14,088
Somewhere, a script types his number into the marketplace web page, again and again.

6
00:00:14,138 --> 00:00:20,696
Who stops it, and who stops the next step, when the script starts to guess the code?

7
00:00:20,746 --> 00:00:26,696
In Lesson 10, the platform made a random code for a registration and kept it only as a digest.

8
00:00:26,746 --> 00:00:36,152
This lesson shows the rules around that code: how long it lives, how many tries it gets, and how many codes one number, one address or one device can ask for.

9
00:00:36,202 --> 00:00:38,024
Start with the code itself.

10
00:00:38,074 --> 00:00:42,320
It is random, with enough digits that a guess is very unlikely.

11
00:00:42,370 --> 00:00:45,968
The platform keeps it in a small table of codes in Postgres.

12
00:00:46,018 --> 00:00:47,840
It does not keep the code.

13
00:00:47,890 --> 00:00:54,344
It keeps a keyed digest of it, made with the code pepper, a secret that lives in Vault, as Lesson 8 showed.

14
00:00:54,394 --> 00:00:57,200
So a copy of the database gives nobody a code.

15
00:00:57,250 --> 00:01:01,184
Each new code also makes the older codes of the same journey void.

16
00:01:01,234 --> 00:01:04,160
So at any moment, one code is alive.

17
00:01:04,210 --> 00:01:10,448
The code is never in clear at rest, not even after it goes to TAF, and it never goes to a log.

18
00:01:10,498 --> 00:01:15,296
When Aziz types a code, the platform compares the digests in constant time.

19
00:01:15,346 --> 00:01:21,440
A wrong code and a nearly right code take the same time to answer, so the timing tells an attacker nothing.

20
00:01:21,490 --> 00:01:23,096
Now the limits on one code.

21
00:01:23,146 --> 00:01:25,520
Each is a policy that the bank sets.

22
00:01:25,570 --> 00:01:27,296
The code has a short lifetime.

23
00:01:27,346 --> 00:01:29,960
It accepts only a few wrong entries.

24
00:01:30,010 --> 00:01:34,280
Then the answer is: wrong code, and the number of tries that remain.

25
00:01:34,330 --> 00:01:40,256
After the last wrong entry, the code is void, and the person must ask for a new one.

26
00:01:40,306 --> 00:01:47,528
A new code needs a minimum interval since the last one: ask too soon, and the answer is wait, then ask again.

27
00:01:47,578 --> 00:01:50,336
And each journey gets only a few resends.

28
00:01:50,386 --> 00:01:52,328
One more limit comes from outside.

29
00:01:52,378 --> 00:01:57,656
TAF has its own lock: after a few wrong codes, it blocks the number for a while.

30
00:01:57,706 --> 00:02:02,792
So the platform's limit on wrong entries may never be looser than the TAF limit.

31
00:02:02,842 --> 00:02:07,616
If it were, TAF would lock Aziz out before the platform even counts.

32
00:02:07,666 --> 00:02:10,064
So guessing one code does not work.

33
00:02:10,114 --> 00:02:12,968
But the script at two in the morning does not guess.

34
00:02:13,018 --> 00:02:14,888
It simply asks for new codes.

35
00:02:14,938 --> 00:02:16,832
That is where the rate limits come in.

36
00:02:16,882 --> 00:02:21,200
The platform counts three things, in the time zone of the installation.

37
00:02:21,250 --> 00:02:23,960
The code requests for each phone number in one day.

38
00:02:24,010 --> 00:02:27,296
The code requests for each network address in one hour.

39
00:02:27,346 --> 00:02:30,560
And the registrations for each device in one day.

40
00:02:30,610 --> 00:02:32,528
The bank sets each limit.

41
00:02:32,578 --> 00:02:35,096
The script hits the limit for Aziz's number.

42
00:02:35,146 --> 00:02:40,136
Its next request gets one answer: too many requests, try again at the given time.

43
00:02:40,186 --> 00:02:45,296
The platform writes an audit event, and sends an event to the bank's antifraud system.

44
00:02:45,346 --> 00:02:47,240
Aziz's phone goes quiet.

45
00:02:47,290 --> 00:02:49,112
Where do these counters live?

46
00:02:49,162 --> 00:02:50,984
In Valkey.

47
00:02:51,034 --> 00:03:00,512
A counter there can carry its own expiry, so a cool-down simply runs out by itself, and a read takes less than a millisecond, even at peak load.

48
00:03:00,562 --> 00:03:03,344
And nothing in Valkey is the only copy.

49
00:03:03,394 --> 00:03:07,664
Each value there either comes again from Postgres, or expires.

50
00:03:07,714 --> 00:03:11,192
I think the most important rule in this lesson is a short one.

51
00:03:11,242 --> 00:03:13,976
A limit in the app is only for convenience.

52
00:03:14,026 --> 00:03:17,264
The app can grey out a button, but only the server counts.

53
00:03:17,314 --> 00:03:20,024
The script at two in the morning never opened the app.

54
00:03:20,074 --> 00:03:24,344
It called the API directly, and the API still said no.

55
00:03:24,394 --> 00:03:29,720
The same idea guards a much more expensive call, which comes later in the course: MyID.

56
00:03:29,770 --> 00:03:38,480
The platform counts failed MyID attempts for three subjects: the phone number, the device, and the personal identification number when it is known.

57
00:03:38,530 --> 00:03:42,032
When one subject reaches a limit, the attempt is refused.

58
00:03:42,082 --> 00:03:47,936
There is a daily cap, a cool-down after the first counted failure, and a longer cool-down after the second.

59
00:03:47,986 --> 00:03:51,920
The refusal tells the person the earliest time of the next try.

60
00:03:51,970 --> 00:03:54,680
Only a failure that the person caused counts.

61
00:03:54,730 --> 00:03:56,792
A face that does not match counts.

62
00:03:56,842 --> 00:03:58,664
MyID being down does not.

63
00:03:58,714 --> 00:04:01,880
An operator can reset the counters, with a reason.

64
00:04:01,930 --> 00:04:03,920
And test customers are never counted.

65
00:04:03,970 --> 00:04:05,792
To recap.

66
00:04:05,842 --> 00:04:10,832
One code, kept as a keyed digest, with a short life and only a few tries.

67
00:04:10,882 --> 00:04:14,336
Rate limits for the number, the address and the device.

68
00:04:14,386 --> 00:04:19,232
Counters and cool-downs in Valkey, and every decision on the server.

69
00:04:19,282 --> 00:04:22,640
But think about the code that Aziz typed correctly on Monday.

70
00:04:22,690 --> 00:04:24,512
It was the right code.

71
00:04:24,562 --> 00:04:27,512
Was it typed on the phone that really holds his SIM card?

72
00:04:27,562 --> 00:04:30,272
Next: the TAF verdict.
